<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armenian genocide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> was the systematic destruction of the Armenian people and identity in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, led by the ruling Committee of Union and Progress (CUP). On 24 April 1915 the authorities arrested and deported hundreds of Armenian intellectuals and leaders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; at the orders of Talaat Pasha an estimated 800,000 to 1.2 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were then sent on death marches to the Syrian desert, where they were robbed, raped and massacred. Around one million were killed, and 100,000 to 200,000 women and children were forcibly converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The Turkish government denies that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> were genocide.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>There the Hamidian massacres of 1894–1896 killed close to 200,000 Armenians, and from the arrests of 24 April 1915 between 1 and 1.5 million were exterminated in the <b>Armenian genocide</b>, which Turkish authorities deny to this day.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Yazidis left for the Russian Empire during the Russo-Turkish wars of 1828–1829 and 1877–1878, and the <b>Armenian genocide</b> of 1915 drove many more from Van, Kars and Bazîd to Transcaucasia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Western Armenian dialects died out as a result of the <b>Armenian genocide</b>, and Western Armenian became the main language of the diaspora and of most Armenian-language schools outside Armenia.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars argue that changing the status of non-Muslims from dhimmi, a subjugated population, to equal subjects clashed with the traditional values of the Muslim population and was in part responsible for the Hamidian massacres and the later <b>Armenian genocide</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars trace the <b>Armenian genocide</b> to long-standing discrimination and land conflicts in the Ottoman east, radicalised by the Balkan Wars, when CUP leaders embraced Turkification and came to see the Christians of Western Armenia as a threat.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Over time the song came to be sung as one of the hymns of the <b>Armenian genocide</b>, which is commemorated every year on 24 April.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dle-yaman">Dle Yaman</a></li></ul></section>
